  "summary": "The health-tech sector is navigating significant regulatory and operational shifts. Neko Health's $700 million funding round underscores continued investor appetite for AI-powered diagnostics, specifically full-body health screening platforms. Meanwhile, Medicare is proposing to ban vendors from providing remote patient monitoring services directly to doctors, a major policy reversal that could reshape how remote care is delivered and reimbursed.\n\nPolitical scrutiny is intensifying around artificial intelligence in healthcare, with bipartisan lawmakers pressing Medicare Advantage insurers about using AI for care denials, while Senate Democrats also grill Trump administration nominees over vaccine skepticism and pandemic preparedness. Supply chain vulnerabilities are drawing attention as lawmakers seek to investigate U.S. reliance on foreign-made pharmaceuticals and ingredients, and Elevance Health is retreating from Medicaid amid persistently high costs.\n\nRecent clinical progress includes improved Alzheimer's diagnostic accessibility, new findings linking Epstein-Barr virus to multiple sclerosis immune responses, and Celcuity's FDA approval of its first breast cancer drug, though healthcare organizations are grappling with complexity in AI implementation and data quality standardization across payers. The Ebola outbreak in Congo continues to worsen with over 2,000 confirmed cases and mounting healthcare worker strikes amid resource constraints.",
